</figure>
<div id="A5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In long-tailed learning scenarios, there exists an inherent trade-off between extremely small <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> (number of samples in the most frequent class) and large imbalance ratio <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> values, since <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{min}=N_{max}/\gamma\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{min}=N_{max}/\gamma\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Our initial experiments on CIFAR-10-LT in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Experimental setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Keep It on a Leash: Controllable Pseudo-label Generation Towards Realistic Long-Tailed Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> already employed challenging settings <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{max}=400,M_{max}=4600,\gamma=100)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4600</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{max}=400,M_{max}=4600,\gamma=100)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{min}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{min}=4</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, even with such extremely few tail class samples, our method still demonstrates significant performance gains over baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further evaluate our method, we conduct additional experiments under three more extreme settings: extreme sparsity setting A1 <math id="A5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{max}=100,N_{min}=1,M_{max}=4900,\gamma=100)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4900</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{max}=100,N_{min}=1,M_{max}=4900,\gamma=100)</annotation></semantics></math>, extreme sparsity setting A2 <math id="A5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{max}=10,N_{min}=1,M_{max}=4990,\gamma_{l}=10,\gamma_{u}=300)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4990</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{max}=10,N_{min}=1,M_{max}=4990,\gamma_{l}=10,\gamma_{u}=300)</annotation></semantics></math>, and extreme imbalance setting B <math id="A5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{max}=400,N_{min}=1,M_{max}=4600,\gamma=300)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4600</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{max}=400,N_{min}=1,M_{max}=4600,\gamma=300)</annotation></semantics></math>. As demonstrated in Table <a href="#A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ E.5 Evaluation under Extreme Settings ‣ Appendix E More Experiment Results and Analyses ‣ Keep It on a Leash: Controllable Pseudo-label Generation Towards Realistic Long-Tailed Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, our method achieves state-of-the-art performance across three settings, attaining 69.56 pp in setting A1, 49.28 pp in setting A2, and 77.78 pp in setting B. These results represent significant improvements over all baseline methods, underscoring our method’s exceptional capability to handle extreme settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.6 </span>Evaluation using Other Architectures</h3>

<figure id="A5.T12" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:27%;">
